  "generated_text": "To determine the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^2} \\), we need to analyze the possible values of the function as \\( x \\) varies over all real numbers.\n\n1. Consider the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^2} \\).\n2. Since \\( x^2 \\) is always positive for all real numbers \\( x \\), the fraction \\( \\frac{1}{x^2} \\) is always positive.\n3. As \\( x \\) approaches infinity or negative infinity, \\( x^2 \\) becomes very large, making \\( \\frac{1}{x^2} \\) approach 0.\n4. As \\( x \\) approaches 0, \\( x^2 \\) approaches 0, making \\( \\frac{1}{x^2} \\) approach infinity.\n5. Therefore, the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^2} \\) can take any positive value.\n\nGiven these observations, the range of the function \\( f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^2} \\) is \\( (0, \\infty) \\).\n\n\\[\n\\boxed{(0, \\infty)}\n\\]",
  "grade": {
    "error": null,
    "extracted_answer": "[Interval.open(0, oo), '(0, \\\\infty)']",
    "gold_answer": "Note that $f(x) = \\frac{1}{x^2} >0$ for all nonzero $x$. That is, the range of $f$ must only include positive numbers. Conversely, if $a$ is a positive number, then \\[f\\left(\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{a}}\\right)=\\frac{1}{(1/\\sqrt{a})^2} = a,\\]so $a$ is indeed in the range of $f$. Thus, the range of $f$ is the set of all positive real numbers; in interval notation, that's $\\boxed{(0,\\infty)}$.",
    "is_correct": true
  },
